[merlyn@digibd.com (Merlyn LeRoy) writes:]
[] I think I've mentioned this before: the addition of the wand of
[] everything changes polypiling.  The WOE zaps as a random wand each
[] time you use it; since it is a polymorphous item, it isn't affected
[] by polymorph.  Repeatedly polymorphing wands will gradually change
[] all of them into wands of everything, since once they change into
[] it, they never change back.  This slows down polypiling to a certain
[] extent, but doesn't eliminate it.

umm, two comments and two questions:

a wand of everything sounds like fun ... is it?

couldn't you achieve the same effect with a polymorphous wand of
nothing? (which is, unfortunately, a more severe penalty)

this approach has some interesting implications: polymorphous items
could be otherwise useless items.  in this way, a polymorph that
changes an item into a polymorphous item would effectively make that
item useless (this is a friendlier implementation of the destruction
theme).  useless or polymorphous items should be distinct from the
so-called "cancelled-type" items.  polymorphous (useless) items:

a wand of nothing
a scroll of mail or a memo
a broken bottle (still a good thing to toss at nasties)
a spellbook of lost spells
a ring of universal size
a wedge (useless tool)
a thimble (useless armor)
a lava stone
a rubber hose

and so on ...

